From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ws2_32: name space lookups, the network list and its change notifications

The Winsock name space service API was a set of stubs: WSALookupService*
failed every lookup, WSAEnumNameSpaceProviders listed nothing,
WSANSPIoctl failed and WSCGetProviderPath returned success with nothing
in the buffer. Programs that find hosts or watch the network this way
(network-aware applications polling NLA for "am I online", older
resolvers, Winsock catalog tools) saw no network at all.

- WSAEnumNameSpaceProviders lists the DNS name space ("Tcpip") and the
  network list ("Network Location Awareness Legacy"), A and W, with the
  size needed when the buffer is short.
- WSALookupServiceBegin/Next/End (A and W):
  - NS_DNS: SVCID_HOSTNAME and SVCID_INET_HOSTADDRBYNAME look a host up
    (the canonical name, every address as a CSADDR_INFO, and for the
    latter a HOSTENT blob with offsets for pointers, as Windows gives
    it); SVCID_INET_HOSTADDRBYINETSTRING takes an address literal;
    SVCID_INET_SERVICEBYNAME gives a SERVENT blob; an unknown host is
    WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND. An empty name is this host.
  - NS_NLA (or no service class): one result per connected network --
    an adapter that is up with an address -- named by its DNS suffix or
    its name, with an NLA_INTERFACE and an NLA_CONNECTIVITY blob
    (managed when the network has a DNS suffix, internet when it has a
    gateway).
  - Results are written as one block (the WSAQUERYSET, then what its
    pointers point at), WSAEFAULT with the size needed for a short
    buffer, LUP_RETURN_* choose the fields, LUP_FLUSHPREVIOUS skips a
    result, then WSA_E_NO_MORE. Bad handles are refused.
- WSANSPIoctl(SIO_NSP_NOTIFY_CHANGE) on a network list lookup: polled
  (WSAEWOULDBLOCK until the networks change) or completed through an
  event, a completion port, a window message or an APC when an address
  changes; ending the lookup completes a pending wait as cancelled. The
  DNS provider refuses it (WSAEOPNOTSUPP), as on Windows.
- WSCGetProviderPath gives the base providers' path
  (%SystemRoot%\system32\mswsock.dll) and WSAEINVAL for an unknown id;
  WSCGetProviderInfo gives their LSP categories (none) and audit record,
  WSANO_RECOVERY for an unknown id.

Wine's ws2_32 protocol tests: 7806 tests, no failures; 29 todo_wine
markers removed (WSALookupService, WSAEnumNameSpaceProviders,
WSCGetProviderPath).

Gate: test/nsplookup-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit, test/nsplookup-probe.c).
Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_BLOB, SG_MUTANT_END_NO_CANCEL,
SG_MUTANT_POLL_ALWAYS_CHANGED (ws2_32/nsp.c).
